        modelAnswer: `• The case for consistency. All liberals begin from the same premise: human beings are rational, self-determining individuals capable of judging their own interests. This is what grounds foundational equality, consent-based government, toleration and the harm principle — if people were not rational, paternalism would be justified and the whole liberal architecture would collapse. The premise is remarkably stable from Locke through Mill to Rawls and Friedan, and Wollstonecraft's argument for women's rights is an application of it rather than a departure. Analysis: this is substantial, since a shared account of human nature is exactly what an ideology is usually asked to supply.\n• The case for inconsistency. Liberals differ sharply over what rationality implies. Classical liberals treat individuals as egoistical — self-interested and self-reliant, best left alone; modern liberals treat them as developmental, capable of flourishing but only under conditions society must supply. Mill sits between the two, defending negative freedom while treating liberty as the condition for individual development. Optimism also varies: Locke's confidence contrasts with Mill's anxiety about the competence of the electorate and his proposal for plural voting, which sits uneasily with equal moral worth.\n• Judgement. The premise is shared and the inference from it is not, which makes this a disagreement about the consequences of a common view rather than about human nature itself. That is a weaker charge of inconsistency than it first appears, but it is not empty: whether reason is something people simply possess or something that requires education, security and health to develop is not a detail — it determines whether the state is liberalism's chief threat or its chief instrument. Liberalism therefore has one consistent view of human capacity and two incompatible views of the conditions under which that capacity is realised, and the second disagreement does most of the political work.`
