        modelAnswer: `• The size of the government majority. A large majority allows a government to control the timetable, carry its business on the payroll vote and absorb rebellions; a small or absent majority transfers leverage to backbenchers and opposition parties. Analysis: this is the most powerful single determinant, and it explains why the same institutions produced very different results in 2015-17 and 2017-19 with no change in the rules whatever. It also reveals the structural weakness: effective scrutiny depends on parliamentary arithmetic rather than on parliamentary design, so it is strongest by accident and weakest exactly when a government is most able to act.\n• Party unity and discipline. A governing party that is divided cannot rely on its majority, and rebellion becomes both more likely and more consequential; whips depend on patronage and loyalty, both of which have weakened. Analysis: this interacts with the first factor rather than operating independently — division matters most when the majority is small. Its significance is that scrutiny is delivered largely by the government's own backbenchers rather than by the opposition, which means it is contingent on the governing party's internal politics rather than on institutional opposition.\n• Institutional resources and independence. Elected select committee chairs, the Backbench Business Committee, urgent questions and the expertise of the Lords all supply scrutiny that does not depend on arithmetic. Analysis: this is the factor most amenable to reform, and the Wright reforms demonstrate that institutional change can shift the balance durably — select committee influence has grown regardless of majority size. The limitation is that these mechanisms expose and embarrass but rarely compel: committees cannot amend legislation and the Lords can be overridden, so they improve the quality of information without altering who decides. The decisive point is that Parliament's scrutiny is strong on publicity and weak on power.`
