        modelAnswer: `• The case that it does. The Lords performs the revising work the Commons lacks the time and incentive to do. Freed from constituencies, re-election and the tightest whipping, and containing former judges, clinicians, scientists, senior officials and independent crossbenchers, it examines legislative detail thoroughly and frequently defeats the government — forcing amendments to bills on issues from welfare reform to counter-terrorism. Since the House of Lords Act 1999 removed most hereditary peers, no party has held a majority, and the chamber has acted with greater confidence. Analysis: this is a real function, and it addresses a genuine defect — the Commons passes legislation under timetabling pressure with a committee stage the government controls.\n• The case against. The chamber is unelected, so it exercises real influence over legislation without accountability to anyone. Appointments are made largely on the recommendation of party leaders, which has produced recurrent controversy about honours and donations, and the chamber had grown past 800 members, with no cap, before the hereditary peers left in 2026. The retention of 92 hereditary peers until 2026, and of the Lords Spiritual still, had no defensible modern justification. Analysis: the objection is not that the Lords works badly but that it has no right to work at all in its present form.\n• Judgement. The Lords performs a useful function and lacks the legitimacy to perform it, and the two facts have a single cause: it is useful precisely because its members are not elected, since that is what supplies the expertise, the independence and the long view. This is why reform has repeatedly failed — every scheme that fixes the legitimacy problem by introducing election also removes the features that make the chamber valuable, and replaces crossbench expertise with professional politicians under party discipline. The defensible conclusion is that the present arrangement is indefensible in principle and superior in practice to the alternatives actually on offer, which is an uncomfortable position but an honest one.`
